    this.target   = target;
    this.text     = text;
    this.opts     = Object.assign({
      fontColor: '#555',          // subtle gray
      fontFamily: 'sans-serif',
      fontSize: 14,
      typingSpeed: 80,            // wpm
      delayBeforeStart: 500       // ms
    }, opts);

    // 1️⃣  Build the SVG and keep its ID for later updates
    this.svgId = this._createSVG();
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     Private: ask the backend to create the SVG and inject it.
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  _createSVG() {
    // The function `createTypingSVG` returns an object that contains the
    // generated SVG markup and a unique identifier.
    const result = createTypingSVG({
      fontColor: this.opts.fontColor,
      fontFamily: this.opts.fontFamily,
      fontSize: this.opts.fontSize,
      text: this.text,
      typingSpeed: this.opts.typingSpeed
    });

    // `result.svg` is the raw <svg> string, `result.id` is the internal ID.
    this.target.innerHTML = result.svg;   // place it in the DOM
    return result.id;
  }

  /** Start (or restart) the animation */
  start() {
    setTypingAnimation({
      delayBeforeStart: this.opts.delayBeforeStart,
      typingSpeed: this.opts.typingSpeed
    });
  }

  /** Change the text on the fly (e.g., after a validation error) */
  update(newText, newOpts = {}) {
    const opts = Object.assign(this.opts, newOpts);
    updateTypingSVG({
      svgId: this.svgId,
      newText: newText,
      newFontColor: opts.fontColor,
      newFontSize: opts.fontSize
    });
    this.text = newText;
  }
}
</script>
```

> **What the wrapper does**  
> * Calls `createTypingSVG` once (so the SVG is generated server‑side).  
> * Stores the returned `svgId` so you can later call `updateTypingSVG`.  
> * Exposes a `start()` method that simply triggers `setTypingAnimation` with the same speed/delay you used when creating the SVG.  

---

## 2️⃣  Mark‑up your form

```html
<form id="signup">
  <div class="field">
    <label for="name">Name</label>
    <input id="name" type="text" autocomplete="off">
    <!-- placeholder for the typing cue -->
    <div class="typing-cue" data-cue="What's your full name?"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="email">Email</label>
    <input id="email" type="email" autocomplete="off">
    <div class="typing-cue" data-cue="Enter a valid email address"></div>
  </div>

  <button type="submit">Sign up</button>
</form>
```

*Each `.typing-cue` element will be turned into an SVG that “types” the instruction text.*

---

## 3️⃣  Wire it up

```html
<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  // Grab every cue container
  const cues = document.querySelectorAll('.typing-cue');

  cues.forEach(cueEl => {
    const text = cueEl.dataset.cue;

    // Create a TypingCue instance for each field
    const cue = new TypingCue(cueEl, text, {
      fontColor: '#888',
      fontSize: 13,
      typingSpeed: 70,          // wpm – feel free to tweak
      delayBeforeStart: 300
    });

    // Find the associated input (assumes the cue is the next sibling)
    const input = cueEl.previousElementSibling;

    // Start the animation when the user focuses the input
    input.addEventListener('focus', () => cue.start());

    // Optional: restart when the user blurs and the field is still empty
    input.addEventListener('blur', () => {
      if (!input.value.trim()) cue.start();
    });
  });
});
</script>
```

### What happens

| Event | Action |
|-------|--------|
| **Page load** – nothing animates yet (keeps the UI clean). |
| **User clicks/focuses an input** – the associated `TypingCue.start()` is called, which triggers the SVG to type the instruction. |
| **User leaves the field empty** – the cue restarts on blur, reminding them what to type. |
| **User starts typing** – the cue stays visible (you can hide it with CSS if you prefer). |

---

## 4️⃣  Styling (optional)

```css
.field {
  position: relative;
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
}
.typing-cue {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  top: 100%;               /* just below the input */
  margin-top: 4px;
  pointer-events: none;   /* let clicks go through to the input */
}
input:focus + .typing-cue svg {
  /* make the cue a little bolder while typing */
  opacity: 1;
}
```

Feel free to adjust `top`, `margin-top`, or even overlay the SVG **inside** the input (by using `position: absolute` and `padding-left` on the `<input>`). The SVG is just regular markup, so any CSS you apply to it works as usual.

---

## 5️⃣  Advanced tweaks

| Goal | How to achieve it |
|------|-------------------|
| **Different speed per field** | Pass a custom `typingSpeed` when you instantiate `new TypingCue(...)`. |
| **Delay before the first cue** | Set `delayBeforeStart` in the options (or call `setTypingAnimation` manually). |
| **Show a “typing completed” checkmark** | After `setTypingAnimation` finishes you can listen for the `animationend` event on the generated `<svg>` (the function returns the SVG element, so you can attach `svg.addEventListener('animationend', …)`). |
| **Replace the cue with a validation message** | Call `cue.update('Looks good! 🎉', {fontColor: '#0a0'})` after a successful validation. |
| **Reuse the same SVG for many fields** | Create one `TypingCue` per *type* of instruction (e.g., “Enter your email”) and call `update()` with the appropriate text before each `start()`. |

